Trace:

  1. joinPoolAndStake computes msgValue = _amounts[i] for whichever leg equals ETH_ADDRESS, then does
  WETH.withdraw(msgValue) and forwards {value: msgValue} into add_liquidity. So the design convention is: the ETH-leg
  amount is held as WETH right up to the Curve call.
  2. The "steals an unrelated WETH bucket and strands native ETH" half of the claim requires a configuration where ASSET
  == address(WETH) (so the vault genuinely holds a separate WETH balance) and the ETH leg is a non-primary leg funded
  by deposit trades. The constructor forecloses exactly that: _PRIMARY_INDEX is set to 0 if TOKEN_1 == ETH_ADDRESS &&
  _asset == address(WETH), and to 1 if TOKEN_2 == ETH_ADDRESS && _asset == address(WETH). Whenever the asset is WETH and
  a leg is ETH, that ETH leg is the primary index. Therefore _amounts[ethIndex] is the primary-side WETH deposit
  remainder — the very WETH the vault holds — and the unwrap consumes precisely that bucket. No foreign WETH is touched,
  nothing is stranded. Conversely, if ASSET != address(WETH) the vault holds no WETH bucket at all, so there is nothing
  to misappropriate. The value-loss branch is structurally impossible.
  3. That leaves only the revert branch: an ETH-legged pool whose asset is neither the ETH leg nor WETH, where the
  secondary ETH leg is bought by _executeDepositTrades. Two problems with elevating this:
    - AbstractSingleSidedLP, _executeDepositTrades, and the trading module are not in the provided source. The claim's
  whole mechanism rests on an asserted buyToken = ETH_ADDRESS → native-ETH delivery behavior in code I cannot read here.
  The unconditional WETH.withdraw is equally consistent with a codebase convention in which the trade leg is
  denominated/settled in WETH before entry (the same convention unstakeAndExitPool re-establishes on the way out when
  the asset is WETH). I cannot point to the exact step that breaks an invariant.
    - Even granting it, the outcome is a deterministic revert of the entry path for a particular deployment
  configuration chosen by a privileged deployer — a leg that never works from block one, discoverable on the first
  deposit, with no unprivileged attacker action, no profit, and no funds at risk. That is a deploy-time
  configuration/functionality defect, not a Medium-severity externally exploitable path.

  The exploit as filed either cannot occur (value-loss variant, blocked by the constructor's primary-index assignment)
  or is an unproven, non-adversarial configuration revert resting on unshown code.

